EXT. FLOODED RICE PADDY DYKE - DAWN

Mist hangs over the water. A broken dyke, a line of mud, a burned-out ox cart half sunk.

CORPORAL HARUTO MIZUNO, twenties, helmet strap loose, sits on the cart's axle cleaning a rifle bolt with a rag. Beside him, PRIVATE EMEKA OBI, nineteen, thin, holds a tin cup of tea with both hands.

OBI
It's quiet.

MIZUNO
It's been quiet since yesterday.

OBI
Is that good?

MIZUNO
It's quiet.

Obi sips. Grimaces.

OBI
This tastes like smoke.

MIZUNO
Everything tastes like smoke.

Mizuno reassembles the bolt, slides it home, works the action once.

OBI
My mother says the fog is the spirits of the rice.

MIZUNO
My mother says it's the river breathing.

OBI
They would get along.

MIZUNO
They would argue.

OBI
(smiling)
Yes. About the rice.

Distant, a cock crows. Both men look up toward the sound.

MIZUNO
That's a good sign.

OBI
Or the farm still has someone in it.

They answer the same moment, quiet.

MIZUNO
We should check.

OBI ^
We should check.

They stand. Mizuno shoulders the rifle. Obi pours the last of the tea into the water and follows him along the dyke into the mist.
